WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:06.310
In a linear algebra course, Matrix inverses
are something that get a lot of attention.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.330
There are all these formulas thrown at you
like for the determinants, the inverses of

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:19.740
a 2x2 matrices, cramner’s rule etc etc.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:25.039
These formulas are great, but I think they
can obscure the very simple idea behind what

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:31.731
an inverse actually is, and when it exists.
You’ve probably been taught that the inverse

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:38.190
exists if and only if the determinant isn’t
0. But most students have no idea what the

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:45.560
determinant has to do with anything let alone
inverses. I’m going to teach a completely

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:52.500
different condition for when an inverse exists,
that I think is much more intuitive.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.060
But before all that:

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.980
Do you know what a linear transform is? If
not, go watch my last video because none of

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:05.239
this one is going to make sense at all otherwise.
In fact, if you want to brush up on vectors

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:14.960
and bases, you can watch the video before
that too.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.159
Even though this video is about matrix inverses,
I’m not going to define what an inverse

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:24.509
is straight away. Instead I’m going to define
something called a left inverse because I

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:32.600
think understanding these first will give
you a better intuition for inverses themselves.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:37.250
Imagine you have a bunch of vectors in some
vector space, in this case in 2d, and you

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:43.950
apply some Matrix M on them, here it rotates
them. Then you think you’d like to undo

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:49.049
what you just did and get the vectors back
to where they were. In this example, what

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:54.899
transformation undoes the rotation? Here,
you’d just rotate everything back by the

00:01:54.899 --> 00:02:00.429
same angle. That’s all a left inverse is.
It’s the matrix that undoes the original

00:02:00.429 --> 00:02:05.309
matrix, so it’s like you’ve done nothing
at all. If I wanted to write this as an equation,

00:02:05.309 --> 00:02:11.849
it’d say, if you do M, then you do L , that’s
the same as if you did nothing. This thing

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:18.230
is called the identity matrix and it just
means the transformation where you do nothing.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:24.319
This thing is called the left inverse for…
hopefully obvious reasons. So now we know,

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:31.950
the left inverse is a matrix that undoes the
original matrix’s action.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.750
The annoying thing about inverses is really
their name. It sounds like the *inverse* should

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:42.320
be the thing that undoes a matrix. Instead
the definition of M inverse is:

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.469
M inverse undoes M

00:02:45.469 --> 00:02:46.469
AND

00:02:46.469 --> 00:02:50.370
M undoes M inverse.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:57.090
Going back to our example: if you do M first
and then L, that’s the identity. But it’s

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:04.219
also true that If you did L first, then M,
you’d also get the identity. So since L

00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:13.540
undoes M and is undone by M, L and M are inverses
of each other. You might wonder, is the left

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:19.260
inverse always also the inverse like this?
No, obviously not, or they wouldn’t have

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:28.219
different names, would they?

00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:32.590
Before we move on, let me ask you a question
to check you’ve understood this so far.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:39.409
Imagine you have a matrix like this. What
it does is that it takes a 3D vector, and

00:03:39.409 --> 00:03:48.319
jumbles up the components. Does this matrix
have a left inverse? As in, can you undo this?

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:53.939
Then if it does have a left inverse, figure
out if it has an inverse as well. Put you

00:03:53.939 --> 00:04:03.709
answer in the poll in the corner, and pause
the video now to think about it.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:09.099
The answer is that it does have a left inverse.
It’s the one that takes a vector like this

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:15.920
and rearranges the components like this. It’s
clear that this is a left inverse of A since

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:27.210
it undoes it like so. But A is also a left
inverse of it, as you can see, because A undoes

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:34.699
this matrix. So B is the inverse of A.

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:40.140
Now that we know what an inverse is, let’s
think more about when they exist or don’t.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:46.210
Again, it’s going to be more convenient
to look at when a left inverse exists first.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:55.461
Here’s another question. This matrix takes
a 2d vector a b and sends it to a 0. Does

00:04:55.461 --> 00:05:01.940
this matrix have a left inverse? If so, figure
out what it is.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:12.229
Again put your answer in the poll in the corner
and pause now to think about it.

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:18.360
Notice something about this matrix. It takes
the vector a b to a 0, but it also takes a

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:24.580
d to a 0 as well. This… is a bad thing,
and it’s because of this that the left inverse

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.410
doesn’t exist.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:33.851
Why? Well, say you have some vector v and
M takes it to w. The left inverse of M, if

00:05:33.851 --> 00:05:42.740
it exists, knows M and what w is, but it doesn’t
know what vector produced w. Just using the

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:48.039
information given, it needs to find what the
original vector was, so that it can take w

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:55.039
back to where it came from. However. If there’s
some other vector u that also goes to w, the

00:05:55.039 --> 00:06:00.099
left inverse has a problem. it can’t just
look at w and know for sure it whether it

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:07.250
came from v or from u because there isn’t
enough information. This means the left inverse

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:14.669
*can’t* take w back to where it came from,
so… it doesn’t exist.

00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:21.680
This thing here, where two different vectors
v and u get mapped to the same vector, i.e

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:28.330
M(u)=M(v), is what I’ll call M losing information.
What we’ve just seen is that if M loses

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:40.100
information it doesn’t have a left inverse.
But what about the other way around? If M

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:46.669
doesn’t lose information, does this mean
the left inverse exists? Well, yes actually.

00:06:46.669 --> 00:06:54.310
Because all the left inverse has to do to
undo M is find the vector w came from. Since

00:06:54.310 --> 00:07:00.289
there’s only one vector v it could be, there
is an inverse that takes w and returns v.

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:07.470
This doesn’t mean it’s easy to find out
what v is necessarily, but looking at w does

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:14.280
in principle give you enough information to
undo M and return v.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:21.979
So a matrix has a left inverse if and only
if it doesn’t lose information.

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:28.409
Let’s look at another example to understand
this point better. Imagine I have a matrix

00:07:28.409 --> 00:07:46.070
from 2d to 3d and what it does is, it rotates
any 2D vector into 3D space like this. Does

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:57.800
this matrix have a left inverse? Pause the
video to think about it.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:02.980
The answer is, it does have a left inverse
because A doesn’t lose information. If you

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:07.159
want to take vectors like this back, you know
where they came from so all you have to do

00:08:07.159 --> 00:08:15.500
is rotate the plane back. Let B be a matrix
that takes 3D vectors to 2D that rotates this

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.550
plane back. It is a left inverse of A.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:27.960
Now, is B the inverse of A? In other words,
Is A B’s left inverse? Pause the video and

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:40.790
think about it.

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:44.061
The answer is, no, B has no left inverse

00:08:44.061 --> 00:08:56.980
We’ll show that by showing B loses information.
First, pick any 3D vector that’s not on

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:08.350
this plane. B has to send it to some 2D vector,
so let’s just say here. But there’s another

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:16.760
3D vector that’s already sent there. It’s
this vector u that’s on the plane. So B(u)

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:24.810
is equal to B(v). Hence B loses information
and doesn’t have a left inverse.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:31.090
There’s an important lesson to be drawn
from this example. You might have wondered

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:41.400
before why we only ever talk about the inverses
of a square matrices. What’s so special

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:49.450
about transformations from n dimensions to
n dimensions? The reason is, non square matrices,

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:57.040
i.e ones from n dimension to m dimensions
never have an inverse. The issue is, if you

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:03.060
have any transformation going from a bigger
space to a smaller space, like B which went

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:10.810
from 3d to 2d, you have to lose information.
These types of matrices always send some vectors

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:13.470
to the same place.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:19.010
If you have a matrix from a smaller to a bigger
dimension, it’s possible that it has a left

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:25.060
inverse- like A did in our example. But it’s
left inverse goes from big to small, like

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:32.470
B, and so it can’t be undone. Hence, even
though some nonsquare matrices have left inverses,

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:37.240
they never have an inverse.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:42.380
Square matrices don’t have these issues
at all though. Actually, for square matrices,

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:49.240
everything massively simplifies because the
left inverse is always equal to the inverse.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:56.580
So if a square matrix has a left inverse,
it automatically has an inverse.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:01.770
I am not going to prove this fact. You are,
for homework. But I will give you an illustrative

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:09.310
example in a little to help you understand
why it’s true.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:14.501
Once you’ve proved it, you’ll see that
for a square matrix A, if B undoes A, then

00:11:14.501 --> 00:11:20.940
A undoes B as well. This gives us an easy
criteria for checking whether A has an inverse

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:26.510
or not, because it’s the same criteria we
used to check whether A has a left inverse:

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:33.890
Just ask, does A lose information? If yes…
then sorry, A inverse doesn’t exist.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:41.390
If no, then A inverse does exist.
But you might be thinking, ok, so what? How

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:46.110
is this easy to check? Wouldn’t you have
to compute the outcome for every single vector

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:51.130
that goes into M and compare the results to
every other vector’s and see if any of them

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:56.300
match? Isn’t that beyond tedious?

00:11:56.300 --> 00:12:01.260
Thankfully there is an easy way to check this
condition. All you need to do is figure out

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:08.820
which vectors get mapped to 0. For any linear
map, 0 is always mapped to 0, but all you

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:14.370
need to check is if there’s any *other*
vector mapped to 0 or not, and that’s enough

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:20.070
to decide if M loses information. Why would
that be enough?

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:27.350
Imagine two vectors u and v do both go to
w, so M loses information. Then the vector

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:37.630
u-v, by linearity, gets mapped to 0. So whenever
you have 2 vectors going to the same thing

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:43.801
like this, you always get at least 2 vectors
ending up at 0. So you can check whether a

00:12:43.801 --> 00:12:49.431
matrix loses information by checking how many
things go to zero. In other words, figure

00:12:49.431 --> 00:12:57.010
out how many vectors v satisfy the equation
Mv=0. This is part of why you spend so much

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:05.540
time in linear algebra courses studying the
solutions to equations like this. You can

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:13.440
solve for v by a) using subsitution b) using
Gaussian elimination or (c) by getting your

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.300
computer to do the Gaussian elimination for
you.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:23.860
But the point is, you can find out if M loses
information easily enough this way, and that

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:29.640
tells you whether M has an inverse.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.590
Let me summarise quickly what we learnt in
this video:

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:40.390
1. A left inverse of a matrix is matrix that
undoes it.

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:47.180
2. That the left inverse exists if you don’t
lose information: i.e, if the matrix never

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:55.040
sends two different vectors to the same vector.
3. The inverse is the matrix that both undoes

00:13:55.040 --> 00:14:04.070
the matrix, and is undone by the matrix
4. Nonsquare matrices never have inverses.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:10.480
5. For a square matrix, the left inverse is
equal to the inverse

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:17.650
6. You only need to check if the square matrix
loses information or not to decide if it has

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:22.200
an inverse.
7. You can check whether the matrix loses

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:27.970
information by looking at how many different
vectors get mapped to 0. This you can do by

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:32.200
solving the equation Mv=0.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:37.120
And so that’s it. But before you run off,
here’s some homework for you. The first

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:43.251
one is multiple choice. Which of these is
the inverse of this matrix? I know that you

00:14:43.251 --> 00:14:47.630
can just check each of these to see which
works, but I’d rather you did it another

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:59.780
way. And for crying out loud, don’t use
the formula for the inverse. Once you’ve

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:03.890
figured it out, put your answer in the poll.

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:09.690
Question 2. Prove that for a square matrix
M inverse is equal to the left inverse of

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:14.910
M. There’s lots of hints in the description
for this, but first I want you to try question

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:18.540
3 because it’s a very illustrative example.

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:26.250
Question 3. Suppose we have the matrix from
before. If we have a left inverse for it,

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:33.630
L, then we know L undoes M. We also know M
takes the basis vectors to these vectors,

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:40.930
so L must take them back. First show that
these two new vectors form a basis.

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:47.570
Then, to show that M undoes L, we need to
show that for any vector v, if you apply L

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:54.260
then M, you get v back. Show this by writing
v as a linear combination of the basis in

00:15:54.260 --> 00:16:00.890
the first part. Hopefully doing this first
will help you with the proof in question 2.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:05.260
As you will have noticed, the first question
was from Brilliant.org. What I like about

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:09.420
questions from there it is that they don’t
just give you a formula and then ask questions

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:13.550
where you plug numbers into that formula.
That’s what I found a lot of highschool

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:18.350
and early university textbooks, and it’s
annoying because that doesn’t teach you

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:23.940
anything. Instead, they get you to do examples
like this one, and understand the principle

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:29.070
yourself, then in the next few question, lead
you to finding the general solution on your

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:31.950
own.

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:36.340
As you can see, this is exactly how I like
to learn new maths- before reading the proof

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:40.380
of anything I’ll do lots and lots of simple
examples first and try and understand the

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:45.700
underlying reasoning, so I love how Brilliant
allows you to do this in a structured way.

